        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A T-tube, in general surgery OSCEs, usually refers to a biliary T-tube (Kehr’s tube) — a flexible drainage tube shaped like the letter T, inserted into the common bile duct in selected cases to provide external biliary drainage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The short transverse limbs lie within the bile duct, while the long stem exits through the abdominal wall and connects to an external drainage bag."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its purposes include draining bile, decompressing the biliary tree and, in selected cases, providing access for postoperative cholangiography or other biliary assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important distinction: T-shaped tubes also exist for other anatomical uses, including tracheostomy and ureteric applications. In a general surgery instrument station, “T-tube” most often means a biliary T-tube unless the context specifies otherwise."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Identification and characteristic features",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/t_tube_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identification: Biliary T-tube / Kehr’s tube."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Characteristic features"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Flexible, hollow tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "T-shaped configuration.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Two short transverse limbs that sit within the bile duct.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "One long external drainage limb.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "External stem connects to a drainage bag or compatible system.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Made from suitable medical-grade silicone, rubber or other specified materials, depending on the model.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Labelling and parts",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/t_tube_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "medizzy.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Transverse limbs",
                      "Lie within the common bile duct and allow bile to enter the tube through its openings."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Long stem",
                      "Carries bile from the biliary tree to the outside."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Drainage lumen",
                      "Provides the pathway for bile drainage."
                    ],
                    [
                      "External connector",
                      "Connects the tube to the prescribed drainage system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "External fixation",
                      "Helps secure the tube and reduce accidental displacement."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Drainage bag",
                      "Collects bile and permits monitoring of its volume and appearance."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact openings and configuration vary by manufacturer. Identify the features actually present on the displayed tube."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "T-tubes can be classified according to their material, size and intended application."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Silicone T-tube: Flexible medical-grade tube used in appropriate drainage applications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rubber T-tube: Traditional design, depending on availability and local practice.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Different calibre T-tubes: Selected according to the anatomy, surgical indication and surgeon's preference.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Biliary T-tube: Used in selected common bile duct procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Other T-shaped tubes: Designed for other anatomical applications; these are not interchangeable with biliary T-tubes.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The term Kehr’s tube specifically refers to the traditional biliary T-tube."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The T-tube provides a route for bile to drain from the common bile duct to an external collection system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its transverse limbs lie within the duct, and the external stem allows bile to leave the biliary tree. Depending on the clinical circumstances, the tube can:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provide temporary external drainage of bile.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reduce pressure in the biliary system.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Permit assessment of bile drainage and output.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provide access for selected contrast studies, such as a T-tube cholangiogram, when clinically indicated.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is not a permanent substitute for normal biliary drainage. The decision to insert and remove a T-tube depends on the surgical findings and the patient's postoperative course."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications and surgical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/t_tube_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Common bile duct exploration"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May be inserted after selected open or laparoscopic common bile duct explorations, particularly when temporary drainage or postoperative access is considered necessary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/t_tube_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Selected cases of choledocholithiasis"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May be used following surgical removal of common bile duct stones when the surgeon considers external drainage appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Selected biliary drainage situations"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May be used when temporary decompression or postoperative evaluation of the biliary tract is required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Modern practice point: T-tubes are not routinely required after every common bile duct exploration. In selected patients, primary duct closure or other approaches may be preferred. The decision depends on duct anatomy, inflammation, residual stones, biliary drainage and the operative findings."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides external biliary drainage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows direct monitoring of bile output.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May help decompress the biliary tree.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can provide access for selected postoperative cholangiography.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be useful in selected complex biliary cases where the surgeon wants temporary access to the duct.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Requires an external tube and drainage bag.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause discomfort and restrict activity.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bile loss may result in fluid and electrolyte disturbances.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires careful tube care and monitoring.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May prolong postoperative management.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can become blocked, displaced or accidentally removed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Removal carries a risk of bile leakage and other complications.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Bile leakage"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bile may leak around the tube or into the peritoneal cavity, potentially causing biliary peritonitis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Excessive bile loss"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Prolonged external drainage can cause fluid depletion and electrolyte disturbances, especially with high output."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Tube blockage or displacement"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The tube may become obstructed by debris or displaced from its intended position, impairing drainage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Infection and cholangitis"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Infection may develop in association with impaired biliary drainage or contamination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "E. Complications following removal"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bile leakage, bile peritonitis and tract-related complications may occur, particularly if removal is undertaken before the tract and biliary drainage are considered safe."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other complications include accidental traction injury, local skin irritation, bleeding and persistent biliary fistula."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Postoperative care and monitoring",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Following insertion, care includes:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Secure the tube: Prevent accidental traction or displacement.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain drainage: Keep the prescribed system patent and positioned according to the clinical plan.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitor output: Record the volume, colour and character of the bile.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assess the patient: Watch for abdominal pain, fever, jaundice, vomiting or signs of peritonitis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitor fluid balance: Replace fluid and electrolytes when clinically indicated.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the insertion site: Check for leakage, infection, skin irritation and dressing problems.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow the surgical plan: Any clamping, flushing, cholangiography or removal must be specifically prescribed.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Safety warning: Do not independently clamp, flush, reposition or remove a biliary T-tube. Unexpected cessation of drainage, new leakage, severe pain or fever warrants prompt clinical assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. T-tube cholangiography",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A T-tube cholangiogram is an imaging study in which contrast is introduced through a suitable biliary T-tube under controlled clinical conditions to visualise the biliary tree."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "It may be used in selected patients to assess:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Patency of the biliary ducts.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Residual common bile duct stones.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Passage of contrast into the duodenum.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Biliary anatomy and selected postoperative abnormalities.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The timing and need for this study depend on the clinical indication and local surgical protocol. It is not mandatory for every patient with a T-tube."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a biliary T-tube, also called a Kehr’s tube, used for temporary external drainage of the common bile duct in selected surgical cases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Why is it shaped like the letter T?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The transverse limbs sit within the bile duct, while the long stem provides a route for external drainage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is its principal indication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected cases following common bile duct exploration, particularly when temporary external drainage or postoperative biliary access is required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is a T-tube cholangiogram?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An imaging study using contrast introduced through a suitable biliary T-tube to evaluate the biliary tree."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What are its major complications?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bile leakage, infection, blockage, displacement, fluid-electrolyte loss and complications following removal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why must the tube not be removed casually?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Premature or inappropriate removal can lead to bile leakage and biliary peritonitis. Removal requires assessment of the surgical circumstances and an appropriate clinical plan."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Is a T-tube required after every common bile duct exploration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Its use is selective, and primary closure or other approaches may be appropriate in suitable patients."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. High-yield examination tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Identify the device as a biliary T-tube or Kehr’s tube when presented in a general surgery station.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Point out the two transverse limbs and the long external drainage stem.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "State that it is inserted into the common bile duct in selected cases.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Mention common bile duct exploration and selected cases of choledocholithiasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Know the purpose of a T-tube cholangiogram.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Prioritise bile leakage, blockage, displacement and fluid loss among complications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Emphasise that clamping, flushing and removal require a prescribed clinical plan.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "t_tube_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. Model OSCE presentation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "t_tube_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a biliary T-tube, also known as a Kehr’s tube. It is a flexible, hollow, T-shaped drainage tube used in selected patients following common bile duct exploration. Its transverse limbs lie within the bile duct, while the long stem exits through the abdominal wall to provide external drainage. Its functions include temporary biliary drainage, decompression and selected postoperative biliary imaging. Complications include bile leakage, blockage, displacement, infection and fluid-electrolyte loss.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}